Gate B2 of Task 3 -- A FULL ROUND COMPLETED WITH THE BROWSER PLAYER IN IT.

Same single run as ../b1/; console.log and server.log here are copies of that
one transcript, kept alongside so this directory stands on its own. ../b1/
carries the commands, the five-verdict count and the Docker UDP-proxy
measurement. The verdict for this directory is the one labelled B3 in the log
-- ../b1/b1.asrun explains the numbering.

B3's reading in full:

  {"rounds_started":2,"rounds_scored":1,"rosters":4,
   "ws_readyState":1,"live_handles":[3],"bound":[[3,1]],"sent":[[3,215]],
   "pending":0,"baseline":{"dropped":0,"sendFailures":0,"dataErrors":8},
   "new_dropped":0,"new_sendFailures":0,"new_dataErrors":0,
   "canvas_w":1024,"canvas_h":768,"gl_err":0,"still_alive":true,"PASS":true}

rounds_started 2 and rounds_scored 1 are the page's own counts of [L]
NEW_ROUND and [L] ROUND_SCORE_TEAM, tapped in the page rather than grepped
afterwards. `sent` went 78 -> 215 datagrams between B2 and B3, so the round
was a live stream on that handle and not a login followed by silence.

NOT [L] ROUND_WINNER, which is what the brief asked for. This build never
prints it on a client: it comes from gGame::Analysis (src/tron/gGame.cpp:3610)
which returns immediately when sn_GetNetState() == nCLIENT. Measured on a
three-round run of this same walk: 0x ROUND_WINNER, 4x NEW_ROUND in the
browser transcript. Waiting for it would have timed out, and `until:` does not
throw, so the PASS after it would still have printed. The substitute needles
and why each one requires a finished round are argued at length in
web/tools/bridge-gate.steps.

THE SERVER'S SIDE OF THE SAME ROUND, from server.log:

  [0] Go (round 1 of 10)!
  [0] Winner: AI team
  [0] Go (round 2 of 10)!
  [0] Gdb core dumped web_user for 3 points.
  [0] web_user lost 2 points since it caused a general protection fault.
  [0] Winner: AI team

The browser player is not a spectator in that log: it has a cycle on the grid
that an AI kills, and the server scores it.

AND THE TWO ENDS AGREE ON THE PACKET COUNTS, which is a better cross-check
than either end's own totals. Both print a per-round summary. The server
(server.log, lines 36-38) says of round 1:

  Time:     13.7395 seconds
  Sent:     15642 bytes in 164 packets
  Received:  6752 bytes in 148 packets

and the client's own console, legible on this directory's screenshot, says of
the same round:

  Time:     13.745 seconds
  Sent:      6456 bytes in 148 packets
  Received: 15980 bytes in 164 packets

148 sent by the client is 148 received by the server, and 164 sent by the
server is 164 received by the client, with no loss in either direction. The
byte totals differ by a constant per-packet amount because the two ends count
different framing, not because datagrams went missing. Neither of these is a
number the gate asserts; they are here because they are the closest thing in
this build to a measurement of the bridge's fidelity over a whole round, and
they say it dropped nothing.

THE SCREENSHOT IS THE START OF ROUND 2, not the middle of round 1, and that is
the useful frame: it shows the browser player's cycle on the grid, the
countdown at 3, and round 1's completed statistics still on the console above
it. ../b1/12-*.png is the same client at the start of round 1, with the HUD
reading "Fastest: web_user", "Enemies: 3 Friends: 1", "Ping: 200 ms".

WHAT THE TWO STEERING KEYPRESSES IN B3 DO NOT PROVE. They exercise the real
input path while a network round is live, and a path that threw there would
stop the round finishing. They do NOT prove a particular turn reached the
server: neither end logs anything per turn, and a round's duration is not a
measurement of one keypress. The browser player lost every round it played
here -- [L] ROUND_SCORE_TEAM -4 web_user for the one round this run scored --
unattended, which is what an untouched cycle against three AIs does. A gate that claimed "it plays well" would be
measuring the wrong thing; this one claims the round completed with the player
in it, and that is what the server's log says.
